The world map in the game client (Phase 28 of the Copilot / Shattered-Maps plan, reworked as a floating window; docs/shattered-maps/surfaces.md). A PRODUCTION feature with no flag: it reads only the audited layer the C# site publishes at /api/world-map/*, and nothing here may import from features/copilot. The window is components/WorldMapWindow.tsx, opened from the Game menu.

api.ts is the HTTP client: every call takes an AbortSignal (a cancelled call answers kind aborted, never an error to show), whole areas are read in 300-room pages and kept in memory, getRoomArea says which area a room is in (how an exit out of an area is followed), and /api/health startedAt is compared every five minutes so a redeployed site drops the memory and refetches past the browser cache. ETag revalidation is the BROWSER's job on purpose: a plain GET has no preflight, and the ETag header is not readable cross-origin without CORS exposure. session.ts places the player: the game's room data has a title and exits and no room number, so the answer is a match (same title, same exit set) in the area the player was last in without any request, else a title search and a comparison of at most four candidate areas; several identical-looking rooms are all returned, it never guesses one. useWorldMap.ts follows game:room-data with one request chain at a time (and exposes the shared client). viewport.ts is the pan/zoom geometry (pure: zoom around a point, fit, centre, where to put the view after a redraw). settings.ts is what the window remembers (other floors shown, tiles, labels).

Gotchas found only in a real browser: capturing the pointer on press retargets the ending click to the stage and breaks tapping an exit, so the window captures only after a 4 px drag (see POINTER-01..03 in docs/standards/edge-cases/pointer-interaction.md). viewport.ts MIN_SCALE is 0.02 on purpose: a large area at fit-to-window needs far less than 0.15, and a higher floor makes Reset view show only part of it. Floors are drawn with a shared frame (align slice), so contentRect() reads data-bounds from the SVG rather than its width and height.

Tests that render anything reaching features/auth/siteApi.ts must mock it (import.meta.env is not supported by the jest transform): see useWorldMap.test.tsx and WorldMapWindow.test.tsx.

Worlds (docs/shattered-maps/worlds.md): world.ts is the registry and names ONE world, the DSL. localWorld.ts is the only file that names the Simulacrum's world and its route; it is imported only behind __SIMULACRUM_ADMIN_LOCAL__ in main.tsx, so a production bundle has neither it nor the string api/world-map/sandbox (check-local-only.mjs fails a production dist that has it). The API client and session are per world, and WorldMapWindow is keyed by the world: never share an area between worlds. Never add a world picker, a world parameter or a second route to production code.

Test seams worth knowing: `__resetWorldMapSessionForTests` (useWorldMap.ts) and `__resetMapWorldsForTests` (world.ts) must both be called between tests that touch worlds, because the shared clients and sessions are per world and live at module level. The window test for a world change spies on `sharedWorldMapApi` and renders WITHOUT an injected `api` (an injected one bypasses the per-world client, so the spy would never be asked). Final showcase shots of the window on real data: playwright/world-map-showcase-real.mjs in the Copilot plan folder.

The player's own rooms live in mine/ (see its .ai-context): sharedWorldMapApi wraps every client with withLayer, so a room a player added is found, placed and drawn like a published one, and the layer is never written into the published map.

sync/ (Phase 44) is the cloud backup of explored and added room ids: backup.ts builds the document for ONE world, posts it to the site (auth/cloudSync.ts), and takes the union back through VisitStore.addMany. It must never run unasked (only the Sync now button calls it), and a room the player added travels as an id only, so another device cannot show it. The document shape and caps are the site's (DSL Server.Dsl/Maps/MapVisitSync.cs); tests are beside the files, the browser and API check is playwright/world-map-backup.mjs in the plan folder.

sync/backup.ts gotcha: tests of anything importing auth/cloudSync must jest.mock('.../auth/siteApi') (import.meta); backup.test.ts and MapBackupSection.test.tsx show the pattern. The window passes its injected visits/layers/backup straight to MapBackupSection so tests never touch the site.

world.ts also holds a hand-picked world on top of the connection's choice (pickMapWorld / useMapWorldChoice, Phase 47+): activeMapWorld() is the pick when there is one, automaticMapWorld() is what the connection chose. The picker (components/MapWorldPicker.tsx) draws nothing with one world, so production has no control; both hooks use useSyncExternalStore with a version number, and setActiveMapWorld must still announce only a change of what is SHOWN (the window restarts on it).

Decision (owner, 2026-11-13): the hand pick is per page and never stored (a reload is automatic again), because it exists for debugging a map rebuilt from another world; do not persist it or make it a setting. Gotcha: a pick wins over the connection, so with a pick set the window keeps showing that world after the connection closes or changes; Automatic is the only way back, and `setActiveMapWorld` must keep announcing only a change of what is SHOWN, or the window restarts for nothing.
